# Who must read a change before it can land.
#
# FR-B14 made code review the security boundary rather than a quality practice:
# every FR-C guarantee rests on all database-touching code having been read by
# someone who knows what it guarantees. OPEN-18 asked who may approve a merge.
# This file is that answer, for the paths where getting it wrong is not
# recoverable.
#
# The list is not "important files". It is two specific kinds:
#
#   1. Things that ARE a guarantee: the schema and its migrations, the grant
#      matrix, the anonymity kernel, the two submission paths, the read path
#      that strips author attributes.
#   2. Things that VERIFY a guarantee: the isolation script, the architecture
#      and kernel tests, the lint boundary rule, the CI workflow. LESSONS.md
#      section 9 records that the verified thing gets read carefully and the
#      instrument gets taken on trust, so the instruments are listed too.
#
# Everything else needs one approval from anyone (branch protection), not from
# a named person.
#
# WHAT WOULD CHANGE THIS. A third developer who knows FR-C well enough to
# review the kernel gets added here, and the bottleneck goes away. Until then
# François is a bottleneck on these paths by choice, because a mistake in them
# publishes rows that can never be unlinked or recalled.

